----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[K: g8-h8]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Let me analyze this position carefully.\n\n**Current Position Analysis:**\n- I'm playing as Black and my king is on g8\n- I only have ONE legal move: Kg8-h8 (king moves from g8 to h8)\n- This is a highly constrained position, suggesting I'm likely in check\n\n**Examining the Position:**\nLooking at the board, I can see:\n- My king (KBF1) is on g8\n- White has a knight on e7 (NWF1) - this is deep in my territory\n- White has another knight on e4 (NWF1)\n- The knight on e7 is likely giving check to my king on g8\n\n**Why only Kg8-h8 is legal:**\nThe knight on e7 can attack g8 (knight moves in L-shape: from e7 it can reach d5, f5, g6, g8, c6, c8). My king must be in check, and h8 is the only escape square that:\n1. Is not attacked by the knight on e7\n2. Is not occupied by my own pieces (f8 has my rook RBF1)\n3. Is not attacked by other White pieces\n\n**Evaluation:**\nThis is a forced move - when you only have one legal move in check, you must take it. Moving to h8 gets my king out of immediate danger from the e7 knight.\n\n**Reason:**\nI am in check from the White knight on e7, and Kg8-h8 is my only legal escape. This is a forced defensive move to get out of check.\n\n**Answer: K: g8-h8**"}
